<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="D-myo-inositol (1,3,4)-trisphosphate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6364" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>|FRAME: INOSITOL-1-4-5-TRISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate| is a secondary messenger molecule used in signal transduction and lipid signaling in eukaryotic cells. It mediates the biological response of a large number of hormones and neurotransmitters in target cells by regulating calcium release from intracellular stores. |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-4-5-TRISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate| is synthesized by hydrolysis of |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYL-MYO-INOSITOL-45-BISPHOSPHA &quot;phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate&quot;| (PIP2), a phospholipid that is located in the plasma membrane, by the action of phospholipase C, as described in |FRAME: PWY-6351 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate biosynthesis|. Once formed, the levels of |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-4-5-TRISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate| are tightly regulated by two mechanisms. In the first mechanism the compound is dephosphorylated to |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-4-BISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4)-bisphosphate| |CITS: [6285891][6095097]|, initiating a pathway that recycles the inositol moiety to the plasma membrane as posphatidylinositols (see |FRAME: PWY-6363 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate degradation|). In the second mechanism, which is described here, |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-4-5-TRISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate| is phosphorylated by inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate 3-kinase to |FRAME: CPD-506 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4,5)-tetrakisphosphate|. This compound has been suggested to have roles in controlling calcium homeostasis, transferring calcium between intracellular stores, and/or regulating calcium entry across the plasma membrane |CITS: [1659392]|. It may also play a role in regulating cross-talk between the calcium and other signaling pathways. A |FRAME: CPD-506 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4,5)-tetrakisphosphate|-binding protein has been identified that can stimulate the GTPase activity of the ras and rap small GTP binding proteins |CITS: [7637787][9038348]|. In fungi and in plants |FRAME: CPD-506 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4,5)-tetrakisphosphate| is phosphorylated further to |FRAME: CPD-1107 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol 1,3,4,5,6-pentakisphosphate| and eventually |FRAME: MI-HEXAKISPHOSPHATE 1D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexakisphosphate| |CITS: [2550825][2548474]|, as described in |FRAME: PWY-6361 1D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol hexakisphosphate biosynthesis I (from Ins(1,4,5)P3)|. However, in mammals |FRAME: CPD-506 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4,5)-tetrakisphosphate| is cleaved to |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-3-4-TRIPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4)-trisphosphate| |CITS: [3010126]|, and synthesis of |FRAME: MI-HEXAKISPHOSPHATE 1D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexakisphosphate| proceeds from there, as described in |FRAME: PWY-6362 1D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol hexakisphosphate biosynthesis II (mammalian)|. |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-3-4-TRIPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4)-trisphosphate| also acts as a link between the degree and duration of receptor-dependent phospholipase C activation (expressed as levels of |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-4-5-TRISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5)-trisphosphate| and thus |FRAME: INOSITOL-1-3-4-TRIPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,3,4)-trisphosphate|) and the generation of other second messenger compounds such as |FRAME: INOSITOL-1456-TETRAKISPHOSPHATE D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5,6)-tetrakisphosphate| and |FRAME: CPD-178 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (3,4,5,6)-tetrakisphosphate| (see |FRAME: PWY-6366 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (1,4,5,6)-tetrakisphosphate biosynthesis| and |FRAME: PWY-6365 D-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol (3,4,5,6)-tetrakisphosphate biosynthesis|).</Description>
